	(text "It;'s important to keep decoupling capacitors\nfor all power pins. You can combine several pins\nino one decoupling capacitor output if they are supa\nclose to eachother.\nBut in general - Datasheet says to have decopling cap\nfor all of them - 1uf and 0.1uF"
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 0 115.57 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 1.2446 1.2446)
			)
			(justify left top)
		)
		(uuid "46cc2a32-0008-4c5b-9d64-0dd9dff36b68")
	)
	(text "FOR UNIPOLAR POWERING SCHEME\nAVSS IS 0 - BASICALY GND\nAS WELL AS DIGITAL GROUND"
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 0 92.71 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 3.1496 3.1496)
			)
			(justify left top)
		)
		(uuid "4fc22700-95d0-41b1-a2e5-5fc608730c22")
	)
	(text "Hi Mojtaba,\n\nI'll try to be more clear.\n\nQ: in total what does the BIASIN pin do and whats it's role?\n\nA: In the event that the BIAS electrode becomes disconnected from the patient,\nby having BIASOUT connected to BIASIIN, the BIASOUT signal can be routed through\nBIASIN and back to the patient through one of the input electrodes. This way, the patient\nstays biased and the measurements stay within the common-mode input range of the PGA.\nOf course, one measurement will be \"lost\" since it's being used to bias the patient.\n\nQ: it should be done in both cascade or daisy chain mode or it's just for daisy chain?\n\nA: it's the same for both modes."
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 0 13.97 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 1.5748 1.5748)
			)
			(justify left top)
		)
		(uuid "53ff0c57-6ef1-4a84-b363-57cf2d9f4b53")
	)
	(text "BIASOUT and BIASIN are shorted, because BIASIN is going\ntowards mux right away. So as i understood if BIASIN is off\nMUX will not use BIAS voltage to shift signal from 0 to\nas i undestood positive AVDD/2. For unipolar powering AVSS\nis equal to 0 and it means it's just GROUND.\nCheck 9.2 Functional Block Diagram for BIASIN"
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 0 64.77 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 1.2446 1.2446)
			)
			(justify left top)
		)
		(uuid "62f22513-1ef1-4c9b-b8be-0e6f0e6a7575")
	)
	(text "here clockselect is DVDD\nto have it set to one to\nhave it later as CLK output\nfor secondary ADC"
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 223.52 162.56 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 1.2446 1.2446)
			)
			(justify left top)
		)
		(uuid "6c9853ae-8455-4db7-b7cd-bc9b4d512bfb")
	)
	(text "This is a very interesting problem! Let me first explain what the SRB\npins do and what the BIAS pin does. The SRB pins are essentially inputs\nfor a reference input that you intend to connect to multiple channels.\nThe SRB2 pin can be connected to individual channels' positive inputs.\nThe SRB1 pin can be connected to all channels' negative inputs.\n\nThe BIAS pin is the equivalent of a right-leg driver for an ECG measurement.\nIt will output a signal that attempts to keep the patient's common-mode\nvoltage within the valid range for the ADC.\n\nThis datasheet was actually just updated with a helpful section that discusses\nthe various functions of these pins. You can find it here starting on page 65.\n\nIn your case, though, since it is intended to be so modular, I recommend\nyou simply connect all INxN inputs to analog ground (assuming you are\nusing bipolar supplies) and find each electrode voltage in a single-ended\nfashion. Then you can average each sample to calculate a \"reference voltage\" to then analyze each individual channel."
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 133.35 19.05 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 1.2446 1.2446)
			)
			(justify left top)
		)
		(uuid "b34d662b-8392-4ee9-a1d4-8b81ab5707a9")
	)
	(text "RANDOM KNOWLEDGE"
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 0 6.35 0)
		(effects
